.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn OSSL_CMP_SRV_process_request
implements the generic aspects of a CMP server.
Its arguments are the
.Vt OSSL_CMP_SRV_CTX
.Fa srv_ctx
and the CMP request message
.Fa req .
It does the typical generic checks on
.Fa req ,
calls the respective callback function (if present) for more specific
processing, and then assembles a result message, which may be a CMP
error message.
If after return of the function the expression
.Em OSSL_CMP_CTX_get_status(OSSL_CMP_SRV_CTX_get0_cmp_ctx(srv_ctx))
yields -1 then the function has closed the current transaction, which
may be due to normal successful end of the transaction or due to an
error.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_CMP_CTX_server_perform
is an interface to
.Fn OSSL_CMP_SRV_process_request
that can be used by a CMP client in the same way as
.Xr OSSL_CMP_MSG_http_perform 3 .
The
.Vt OSSL_CMP_SRV_CTX
must be set as
.Em transfer_cb_arg
of
.Fa client_ctx .
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_CMP_SRV_CTX_new
creates and initializes an
.Vt OSSL_CMP_SRV_CTX
structure associated with the library context
.Fa libctx
and property query string
.Fa propq ,
both of which may be NULL to select the defaults.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_CMP_SRV_CTX_free
deletes the given
.Fa srv_ctx .
If the argument is NULL, nothing is done.
.Pp
.Xr OSSL_CMP_SRV_CTX_init 3
sets in the given
.Fa srv_ctx
a custom server context pointer as well as callback functions performing
the specific processing of CMP certificate requests, revocation
requests, certificate confirmation requests, general messages, error
messages, and poll requests.
All arguments except
.Fa srv_ctx
may be NULL. If a callback for some message type is not given this means
that the respective type of CMP message is not supported by the server.
.Pp
.Xr OSSL_CMP_SRV_CTX_init_trans 3
sets in
.Fa srv_ctx
the optional callback functions for initiating delayed delivery and
cleaning up a transaction.
If the <delay> function is NULL then delivery of responses is never
delayed.
Otherwise
.Em delay
takes a custom server context and a request message as input.
It must return 1 if delivery of the respective response shall be
delayed, 0 if not, and -1 on error.
If the <clean> function is NULL then no specific cleanup is performed.
Otherwise
.Em clean
takes a custom server context and a transaction ID pointer as input,
where the pointer is NULL in case a new transaction is being started and
otherwise provides the ID of the transaction being terminated.
The <clean> function should reset the respective portions of the state
and free related memory.
It must return 1 on success and 0 on error.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_CMP_SRV_CTX_get0_cmp_ctx
returns the
.Vt OSSL_CMP_CTX
from the
.Fa srv_ctx .
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_CMP_SRV_CTX_get0_custom_ctx
returns the custom server context from
.Fa srv_ctx
that has been set using
.Xr OSSL_CMP_SRV_CTX_init 3 .
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_CMP_SRV_CTX_set_send_unprotected_errors
enables sending error messages and other forms of negative responses
unprotected.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_CMP_SRV_CTX_set_accept_unprotected
enables acceptance of requests without protection of with invalid
protection.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_CMP_SRV_CTX_set_accept_raverified
enables acceptance of ir/cr/kur messages with POPO 'RAVerified'.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_CMP_SRV_CTX_set_grant_implicit_confirm
enables granting implicit confirmation of newly enrolled certificates if
requested.
.Sh NOTES
CMP is defined in RFC 4210 (and CRMF in RFC 4211).
.Pp
So far the CMP server implementation is limited to one request per CMP
message (and consequently to at most one response component per CMP
message).
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn OSSL_CMP_SRV_CTX_new
returns a
.Vt OSSL_CMP_SRV_CTX
structure on success, NULL on error.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_CMP_SRV_CTX_free
does not return a value.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_CMP_SRV_CTX_get0_cmp_ctx
returns a
.Vt OSSL_CMP_CTX
structure on success, NULL on error.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_CMP_SRV_CTX_get0_custom_ctx
returns the custom server context that has been set using
.Xr OSSL_CMP_SRV_CTX_init 3 .
.Pp
All other functions return 1 on success, 0 on error.
.Sh HISTORY
The OpenSSL CMP support was added in OpenSSL 3.0.
.Pp
.Xr OSSL_CMP_SRV_CTX_init_trans 3
supporting delayed delivery of all types of response messages was added
in OpenSSL 3.3.
